WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
Every event matters.

2
00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:05.800
But in distributed systems, reliability isn’t guaranteed.

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:07.540
Transactions fail.

4
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.120
Messages get lost.

5
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:10.780
Systems go down.

6
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.600
The transactional outbox pattern solves part of the problem.

7
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.100
But what about ordering, retries, scaling,

8
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.960
and everything that happens after the commit?

9
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.140
That’s where Namastack comes in.

10
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.340
Namastack Outbox brings production-grade event delivery directly into Spring Boot.

11
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:29.840
Atomic persistence.

12
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:31.420
Reliable retries.

13
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.300
Strict per-key ordering.

14
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:34.680
Horizontal scaling.

15
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.400
Built-in observability.

16
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:37.580
Multiple databases.

17
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.660
Multiple messaging systems.

18
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.620
No fragile workarounds.

19
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.600
No infrastructure maze.

20
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.880
Just reliable event delivery.

21
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.860
Designed for the real world.

22
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:49.340
Namastack Outbox.

23
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:50.800
Built for reliability.

24
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.460
Designed for scale.

25
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:53.600
Open source.

26
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.280
Built for Spring Boot.
